<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azemmour</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Azemmour is a town on the Oum Er-Rbia river near its mouth on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>. Portugal took it in 1513 as one of its chain of fortified posts on the coast, but abandoned it in 1541 after the Saadi capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agadir">Agadir</a>. Its walled medina still overlooks the river.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/azemmour.jpg" alt="Azemmour" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yaroslav Blanter · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Azemmour_from_Oum_Er-Rbia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…sixteenth century Morocco was divided: the Wattasid sultans at Fez controlled only the north, local lords and religious brotherhoods held much of the rest, and the Portuguese had seized a chain of Atlantic ports including Safi, <b>Azemmour</b> and Agadir, then known as Santa Cruz do Cabo de Gué.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saadi-sultanate">Saadi Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They dealt with the Portuguese posts at Safi and <b>Azemmour</b> and are described by Leo Africanus, who visited in the early 16th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/hintata-emirate">Hintata emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>Muhammad al-Ayyashi, a Sufi disciple appointed as governor near <b>Azemmour</b>, broke with the weak Saadi court and around 1614 began leading tribal holy war against the Christian forts, based at Salé.</blockquote><a href="/state/marabout-state-of-al-ayyashi">Marabout state of al-Ayyashi</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Manuel I the Portuguese built or seized Atlantic posts at Agadir, Safi, <b>Azemmour</b> and Mazagan between 1505 and 1514.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-morocco">Portuguese Morocco</a></li></ul></section>
